WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.585
Three deliveries, plus four. Where does seven go?

00:00:03.755 --> 00:00:09.045
Pip's tiny demonstration program must add two values and save
the result.

00:00:09.215 --> 00:00:11.861
The processor follows instruction meanings

00:00:11.971 --> 00:00:19.224
and changes stored state. It does not understand the whole
program like a reader understands a story.

00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:23.810
Have you ever wondered how a line of code becomes a visible
result?

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:30.999
Chapter one, The Fundamentals, lets us trace the registers and
follow every important state change.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.910
You will explain fetch, decode, and execute, and give each
teaching-model register a clear job.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.883
You will trace four instructions and distinguish an immediate
value from a memory address.

00:00:46.053 --> 00:00:49.082
You will also connect source code, translators,

00:00:49.192 --> 00:00:55.571
and machine instructions without oversimplifying how modern
language implementations work.

00:00:55.741 --> 00:01:00.456
Welcome to the tutorial on program execution by Blue Impulse.

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:04.615
Have a small table ready with columns for the program counter,

00:01:04.725 --> 00:01:09.482
current instruction, accumulator, and memory location two
hundred.

00:01:11.628 --> 00:01:15.596
The subtopic From source code to execution explains that

00:01:15.706 --> 00:01:20.229
machine code contains the instructions a processor executes.

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:23.215
An assembler translates assembly mnemonics

00:01:23.325 --> 00:01:30.045
and labels. A compiler produces another representation, often
native code or bytecode.

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:35.911
An interpreter runs operations through a runtime, sometimes
working with bytecode.

00:01:36.081 --> 00:01:40.689
The same language can have compiled and interpreted
implementations,

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:44.127
and modern systems may combine several techniques.

00:01:44.297 --> 00:01:50.654
Avoid the blanket claim that every interpreter translates
exactly one source line at a time.

00:01:50.824 --> 00:01:58.142
Our demonstration uses invented assembly notation so that every
state change remains easy to see.

00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:07.605
Use a recipe to remember the register jobs. The program counter
marks the next recipe line.

00:02:07.775 --> 00:02:15.668
The memory address register selects the location. The memory
data register carries the item read from memory.

00:02:15.838 --> 00:02:19.806
The current instruction register holds the instruction being

00:02:19.916 --> 00:02:24.652
followed. The accumulator is a temporary mixing bowl for
arithmetic.

00:02:24.822 --> 00:02:31.414
We abbreviate those names as P C, M A R, M D R, C I R, and A C
C.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:39.542
The analogy helps you remember the jobs, but these are
electronic storage locations inside a processor model.

00:02:39.712 --> 00:02:43.829
Keep the jobs distinct: an address points to a location,

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:47.971
while an instruction or value is the content being handled.

00:02:50.759 --> 00:02:57.607
Follow the fetch sequence. First copy the address in the program
counter to the memory address register.

00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:03.046
Read the instruction at that memory location into the memory
data register.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:08.336
Advance the program counter so it identifies the next
instruction address,

00:03:08.446 --> 00:03:13.097
then copy the fetched instruction into the current instruction
register.

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:21.075
The control unit can now decode the operation and its operand,
and the processor executes the required work.

00:03:21.245 --> 00:03:28.882
In this simplified model, every instruction occupies one word,
so the program counter advances by one.

00:03:29.052 --> 00:03:36.476
Real instruction sizes and exact timing depend on the
architecture. Our table is a teaching model,

00:03:36.586 --> 00:03:39.423
not every processor's wiring diagram.

00:03:39.593 --> 00:03:45.332
An address identifies a place. A value is the information stored
or processed

00:03:45.442 --> 00:03:51.010
there. Keeping these roles separate makes the register trace
much easier to follow.

00:03:54.411 --> 00:04:02.048
In the subtopic Trace a tiny program, the first instruction sits
at address one hundred: LOAD, hash three.

00:04:02.218 --> 00:04:07.168
The hash sign denotes an immediate value in this invented
notation,

00:04:07.278 --> 00:04:11.139
so that instruction puts the number three into the accumulator.

00:04:11.309 --> 00:04:19.074
Address one hundred and one contains ADD, hash four. Address one
hundred and two contains STORE, two hundred.

00:04:19.244 --> 00:04:24.343
Address one hundred and three contains HALT, which ends this
teaching program.

00:04:24.513 --> 00:04:32.641
The number two hundred in STORE identifies a memory location. It
is not the value to put into the accumulator.

00:04:32.811 --> 00:04:39.104
Before running anything, predict what that memory location will
contain when the program finishes.

00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:48.076
Let's single-step. Initially, the program counter is one
hundred. Fetch the LOAD instruction

00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:50.426
and advance to one hundred and one.

00:04:50.596 --> 00:04:56.036
Decode and execute LOAD, hash three. The accumulator now
contains three.

00:04:56.206 --> 00:05:04.099
Next fetch ADD, hash four from address one hundred and one. The
program counter advances to one hundred and two.

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:07.554
Execution adds four to the accumulator's three,

00:05:07.664 --> 00:05:12.208
giving seven. Memory location two hundred has not been written
yet.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:17.178
Fetch STORE, two hundred. The program counter advances to one
hundred

00:05:17.288 --> 00:05:22.387
and three, and execution copies seven into memory location two
hundred.

00:05:22.557 --> 00:05:30.557
The accumulator still contains seven. STORE copies the value; it
does not turn the accumulator into an address.

00:05:30.727 --> 00:05:34.076
Finally fetch HALT. In this simplified trace,

00:05:34.186 --> 00:05:39.541
the fetch advances the counter to one hundred and four before
execution stops.

00:05:39.711 --> 00:05:42.932
The runnable Python model prints these same states

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:47.586
and register transfers, so you can reproduce the trace yourself.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.184
Pause and check your prediction. After the STORE instruction,

00:05:55.294 --> 00:05:59.134
what are the accumulator and memory location two hundred?

00:05:59.304 --> 00:06:04.680
Also ask yourself whether STORE, two hundred means storing the
number two hundred.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.856
The accumulator remains seven,

00:06:11.966 --> 00:06:18.259
and memory location two hundred receives seven. Two hundred is
the destination address.

00:06:18.429 --> 00:06:26.152
The hash sign in our LOAD and ADD operands marks literal values.
The STORE operand has a different role.

00:06:26.322 --> 00:06:33.660
This distinction prevents a common tracing mistake: confusing
where data lives with the value stored there.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.436
Use this model's instruction meanings before applying

00:06:37.546 --> 00:06:41.450
assumptions from a different programming language or processor.

00:06:44.498 --> 00:06:50.044
A branch instruction may replace the normal next value of the
program counter.

00:06:50.214 --> 00:06:57.745
That is how the instruction sequence can follow a decision or
repeat a loop instead of always moving forward.

00:06:57.915 --> 00:07:04.720
Our four-instruction program contains no branch, so the normal
next addresses stay easy to follow.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:11.056
Chapter three, Hardware, adds pipelining: real processors may
overlap instructions

00:07:11.166 --> 00:07:14.302
while preserving the required program behaviour.

00:07:14.472 --> 00:07:20.936
Overlapping the implementation does not make the logical effects
of a program arbitrary.

00:07:21.106 --> 00:07:25.799
For a basic trace, first understand the defined instruction
effects,

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:29.386
then study how a specific processor implements them.

00:07:31.976 --> 00:07:39.315
Your turn. Change ADD, hash four to ADD, hash nine, while
keeping LOAD, hash three and STORE, two hundred.

00:07:39.485 --> 00:07:45.991
What value should reach memory location two hundred? Work
through the accumulator before we reveal it.

00:07:52.161 --> 00:07:59.564
LOAD gives three. Adding nine gives twelve. STORE copies twelve
into memory location two hundred.

00:07:59.734 --> 00:08:05.665
That is a small example of testing a prediction by changing one
input to the program.

00:08:05.835 --> 00:08:10.677
The included simulator accepts a different add value, prints the
trace,

00:08:10.787 --> 00:08:15.161
and checks that the final memory result matches the arithmetic.

00:08:15.331 --> 00:08:22.563
Try another value and explain each register's role aloud as the
instruction moves through the cycle.

00:08:25.715 --> 00:08:29.769
Recap the lesson. Source code reaches execution through

00:08:29.879 --> 00:08:34.295
a translator or runtime appropriate to its implementation.

00:08:34.465 --> 00:08:40.395
The program counter identifies the next instruction. Fetch
brings it from memory,

00:08:40.505 --> 00:08:44.729
decode interprets it, and execute performs its operation.

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:51.129
The memory address register selects a location, the memory data
register carries data,

00:08:51.239 --> 00:08:54.993
and the current instruction register holds the operation.

00:08:55.163 --> 00:09:02.267
Our teaching accumulator went from three to seven, and STORE
copied seven to memory location two hundred.

00:09:02.437 --> 00:09:09.264
Addresses and values have different roles. Branches can change
the normal next instruction address.

00:09:09.434 --> 00:09:16.709
Complete the trace without watching the highlight. Understanding
means predicting each state change yourself.

00:09:16.879 --> 00:09:19.204
Pip's seven is now a stored result

00:09:19.314 --> 00:09:24.114
because STORE copied the accumulator value to the chosen memory
address.

00:09:24.224 --> 00:09:28.725
The instruction determined both what changed and what stayed the
same.

00:09:32.498 --> 00:09:36.252
Pip's small problem now has an explanation you can test.

00:09:36.422 --> 00:09:42.225
Use Chapter 1, The Fundamentals, and the named subtopics to
revisit the reasoning.

00:09:42.395 --> 00:09:45.808
The tutorial guide includes the full teaching steps

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:51.678
and the checked practice answers. Run the supporting
demonstration and change one input.

00:09:51.848 --> 00:09:57.182
Blue Impulse. Follow the signal, predict the result, and check
your explanation.
